        <review>&lt;p&gt;Framed as a quiet dialogue between Marco Polo and an aging Kublai Khan, &lt;em&gt;Invisible Cities&lt;/em&gt; offers fifty-five brief, jewel-like descriptions of impossible cities — cities of memory, mirrors, thin air, and hidden geometry.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Best savored a few pages at a time, like poetry or an architectural sketchbook.&lt;/p&gt;</review>
